  "mechanisms": [
    "Irrelevant sound effect: background sounds, especially speech, disrupt serial recall and other cognitive tasks.",
    "Changing State hypothesis: disruption is caused by variations in sound over time (changing-state properties), not necessarily by speech-like qualities or semantic content.",
    "Lack of habituation: short periods of quiet spontaneously restore interference, or workers are not actively attending to the irrelevant sound.",
    "Cognitive load: disruptive sounds increase cognitive load, impairing concentration and performance."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Position in company (job category)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Divert unanswered telephones to a central answering service.",
    "Quiet noisy office equipment.",
    "Carefully plan office layouts to locate quiet-task workers away from noisy equipment or co-workers.",
    "Use sound absorbing materials on ceilings and floors to dampen overall ambient sound and make speech less intelligible, reducing segmentation cues.",
    "Adding continuous noise (noise perfuming) to mask speech is theoretically promising but has had indifferent results in practice due to occupant complaints.",
    "The provision of enforced quiet areas was rated least popular by employees."
  ],
